      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{62e218d37a0d3a44c48bbdbd|full_name}: I recommend googling it for the best explanation. But in short when computers (and servers) talk to each other compared to people talking, packets drop is when not all the words of the speaker arrive to the listeners.\nThere are many reasons why this can happen; bad cables, bad Wifi, Wifi interference, computer being to slow to handle all the incoming packets, …\nThe easiest way to deal with this is making software to deal with it. You will rarely see a packet drop issue on YouTube and on Twitch you see it as the stream buffering and Twitch suggestions to disable low latency mode so your computer and Twitch server have time to resend the packet befor it needs it to display to you.\nYou could attempt to fix it on your own side (I mean by that your home network). First thing to do is use ethernet cables. Second thing is more work and potentially more difficult, checking your cables. You will need tools for this and the better the more expensive they are. Google this for the right info. DIY ethernet testers are 10-20€ and you can also use 2 computers.\nThere is also a possibility that the issue is not on your side but your ISP (their network connects you and other houses to the internet). If this is the case your internet is most likely slow because if the ISP cared it wouldn’t. You can complain to them is that is the case, or maybe even ask help about this in general.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
